/* book: lead: Network synthesis is analysis run backwards. Analysis finds the driving-point impedance Z(s) of a given circuit; synthesis starts with Z(s), asks whether passive R, L and C can produce it, and builds the circuit. Z(s) must be positive real, and for LC or RC networks its poles and zeros must follow a strict pattern. Foster's method then builds the network by partial fractions and Cauer's by continu */
/* book Figure 6.8.1: The LC impedance of the worked example: its poles (×) and zeros (○) alternate along the jω axis, so it is realizable. Partial fractions give the Foster I chain and long division gives the Cauer I ladder; both have four elements. */
