/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A driving-point impedance Z(s) = V(s)/I(s) is measured at one port, with zero initial conditions; the driving-point admittance is Y(s) = 1/Z(s).
   - Z(s) can be built from passive elements only if it is positive real (PR): (1) Z(s) is real when s is real, and (2) Re Z(s) ≥ 0 whenever Re s ≥ 0.
   - The degrees of numerator and denominator differ by at most one.
   - An LC (lossless, reactance) function is an odd rational function: even polynomial over odd, or odd over even, with degrees differing by exactly one.
   - where k∞, k₀ and every kᵢ are positive (or zero for the first two).
   - An RC impedance has simple, alternating poles and zeros on the negative real axis (s = −σ).
   - Foster I expands Z(s) in partial fractions: the terms are elements in series.
   - Cauer I removes the pole at infinity again and again by long division with the polynomials in descending powers.
*/
