/* book Worked example: Q: Show that Z(s) = (s² + 1)(s² + 3)/[s(s² + 2)] is an LC impedance and realize it in Foster I, Foster II, Cauer I and Cauer II forms.
   1. Poles: s = 0, ±j√2 (1.414) and s = ∞ (numerator degree 4, denominator 3). Zeros: ±j1 and ±j√3 (1.732). Along the jω axis: pole 0, zero 1, pole 1.414, zero 1.732, pole ∞. They alternate, all are simple, and Z is even over odd: an LC impedance.
   2. Foster I. k∞ = 1 (leading coefficients). k₀ = [sZ(s)] at s = 0 = 3/2. At s² = −2: (s² + 2)Z/s = (s⁴ + 4s² + 3)/s² = (4 − 8 + 3)/(−2) = 1/2 = 2k₁. So Z = s + 1.5/s + 0.5s/(s² + 2).
   3. Foster I elements, all in series: L = 1 H, C = 1/1.5 = 2/3 F, and a tank with C = 1/0.5 = 2 F and L = 0.5/2 = 1/4 H.
   4. Foster II. Y = s(s² + 2)/[(s² + 1)(s² + 3)] = 0.5s/(s² + 1) + 0.5s/(s² + 3) (residues 1/2 at s² = −1 and at s² = −3). Two series LC branches in parallel: L = 1/0.5 = 2 H with C = 0.5/1 = 1/2 F, and L = 2 H with C = 0.5/3 = 1/6 F.
   5. Cauer I (descending powers): (s⁴ + 4s² + 3) ÷ (s³ + 2s) gives s, remainder 2s² + 3 → series L₁ = 1 H. (s³ + 2s) ÷ (2s² + 3) gives s/2, remainder s/2 → shunt C₂ = 1/2 F. (2s² + 3) ÷ (s/2) gives 4s, remainder 3 → series L₃ = 4 H. (s/2) ÷ 3 gives s/6 → shunt C₄ = 1/6 F.
   6. Cauer II (ascending powers): (3 + 4s² + s⁴) ÷ (2s + s³) gives 3/(2s), remainder 2.5s² + s⁴ → series C₁ = 2/3 F. (2s + s³) ÷ (2.5s² + s⁴) gives 4/(5s), remainder 0.2s³ → shunt L₂ = 5/4 H. (2.5s² + s⁴) ÷ 0.2s³ gives 25/(2s), remainder s⁴ → series C₃ = 2/25 F. 0.2s³ ÷ s⁴ gives 1/(5s) → shunt L₄ = 5 H.
   7. Check: at s = j1 Foster I gives j(1 − 1.5 + 0.5) = 0, the expected zero at ω = 1 rad/s.
   Answer: Foster I: 1 H, 2/3 F and a 1/4 H ∥ 2 F tank in series. Foster II: (2 H, 1/2 F) and (2 H, 1/6 F) series branches in parallel. Cauer I: series 1 H, shunt 1/2 F, series 4 H, shunt 1/6 F. Cauer II: series 2/3 F, shunt 5/4 H, series 2/25 F, shunt 5 H. Each uses four elements. */
