/* book exam traps: Positive coefficients alone do not make a function PR or LC. Z(s) = (s² + 4)/[s(s² + 1)] has positive coefficients, but its critical frequencies run pole 0, pole 1, zero 2; its residue at ±j1 is −3, so it is not an LC impedance. | (s² + 4)/(s² + 1) is not an LC impedance: it is even, and Re Z(jω) < 0 between 1 and 2 rad/s, so it is not even PR. | Foster I expands Z(s) into series elements; Foster II expands Y(s) into parallel branches. Expanding Y but drawing series elements gives the dual circuit, not the asked one. | In Foster I a term k₀/s is a capacitor of 1/k₀, not k₀: 1.5/s is 2/3 F. | For an RC impedance the critical frequency nearest the origin must be a pole. (s + 1)/(s + 2) has a zero nearest the origin, so it is an RL impedance (or an RC admittance), not an RC impedance. */
